{
  "id": "00-sounds",
  "level": "A1",
  "title": "Sounds of German",
  "description": "Train your ear and mouth before your first words.",
  "icon": "🗣️",
  "vocab": [],
  "grammarNotes": [
    {
      "id": "sounds-cheat-sheet",
      "title": "Pronunciation cheat sheet",
      "body": "German spelling is very regular: once you know these rules, you can pronounce almost any word you read.",
      "table": [
        ["Spelling", "Sounds like", "Example"],
        ["ei", "eye", "drei, mein"],
        ["ie", "ee (see)", "vier, Liebe"],
        ["eu / äu", "oy (boy)", "neu, Häuser"],
        ["au", "ow (how)", "Haus, Frau"],
        ["ä", "e in bed", "Käse"],
        ["ö", "ay with rounded lips", "schön"],
        ["ü", "ee with rounded lips", "Tschüss"],
        ["w", "v", "Wasser"],
        ["v", "f (usually)", "Vater"],
        ["z", "ts (cats)", "Zeit"],
        ["s + vowel", "z (zoo)", "sieben"],
        ["sp / st at the start", "shp / sht", "sprechen, Straße"],
        ["ch after a, o, u, au", "Scottish loch", "Buch, auch"],
        ["ch elsewhere", "soft hiss (huge)", "ich, nicht"],
        ["-er at the end", "a in sofa", "Vater"],
        ["b, d, g at the end", "p, t, k", "Tag, Hund"],
        ["ß", "ss", "Straße"]
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "sounds-stress",
      "title": "Word stress",
      "body": "Most German words stress the **first** syllable: *MOR-gen, HA-ben, AR-bei-ten*.\n\n- The prefixes *be-, ge-, er-, ver-, zer-, ent-* are never stressed: *be-SU-chen, ver-STE-hen*\n- Many borrowed words stress the end: *Mu-SIK, Ho-TEL*"
    }
  ],
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "s1-vowels",
      "title": "Vowels and umlauts",
      "kind": "SOUNDS",
      "introduces": [],
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "s1-e1",
          "type": "TEACH",
          "title": "Pure vowels, long and short",
          "body": "German vowels don't glide like English ones — keep your mouth still while you say them.\n\nEvery vowel can be **long** or **short**. It's usually long before a single consonant or an *h* (*Name, Sohn*) and short before two consonants (*Mann, Sonne*).",
          "examples": [
            { "text": "Name", "translation": "name", "hint": "long a" },
            { "text": "Mann", "translation": "man", "hint": "short a" },
            { "text": "Staat", "translation": "state", "hint": "long a" },
            { "text": "Stadt", "translation": "city", "hint": "short a" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e2",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "instruction": "Long or short? Which word do you hear?",
          "options": ["Staat", "Stadt"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e3",
          "type": "TEACH",
          "title": "Umlauts: ä, ö, ü",
          "body": "The two dots change the sound:\n\n- **ä** sounds like the *e* in *bed*\n- **ö**: say *ay* (as in *say*) and round your lips as if to whistle\n- **ü**: say *ee* and round your lips into an *oo*\n\nUmlauts often mark a plural: *Mutter → Mütter* (mother → mothers).",
          "mouthShapes": [
            { "shape": "SPREAD", "label": "ee", "caption": "Lips wide, like a smile" },
            { "shape": "TIGHT_ROUNDED", "label": "ü", "caption": "Keep the ee, push lips into a tight circle" },
            { "shape": "HALF_OPEN", "label": "ay / ä", "caption": "Relaxed, jaw half open" },
            { "shape": "ROUNDED", "label": "ö", "caption": "Keep the ay, round your lips" }
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "text": "Käse", "translation": "cheese" },
            { "text": "schön", "translation": "beautiful, nice" },
            { "text": "Tschüss", "translation": "bye" },
            { "text": "Mütter", "translation": "mothers" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e4",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["schon", "schön"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e5",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Mutter", "Mütter"],
          "answerIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e6",
          "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
          "instruction": "Quick check",
          "prompt": "How do you make the ü sound?",
          "promptLanguage": "BASE",
          "options": [
            "Say ee with your lips rounded like oo",
            "Say oo with a wide smile",
            "Say u as in cup"
          ],
          "optionsLanguage": "BASE",
          "answerIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e7",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Bruder", "Brüder"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e8",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Apfel", "Äpfel"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e9",
          "type": "SHADOW",
          "text": "Sehr schön!",
          "translation": "Very nice!",
          "hint": "For ö, get ready to say ay, then round your lips.",
          "mouthShapes": [
            { "shape": "ROUNDED", "label": "ö", "caption": "Say ay, then round your lips" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s1-e10",
          "type": "SHADOW",
          "text": "Tschüss!",
          "translation": "Bye!",
          "hint": "Round your lips tightly for the ü.",
          "mouthShapes": [
            { "shape": "TIGHT_ROUNDED", "label": "ü", "caption": "Say ee with lips in a tight circle" }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "s2-diphthongs",
      "title": "ei, ie, eu and au",
      "kind": "SOUNDS",
      "introduces": [],
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "s2-e1",
          "type": "TEACH",
          "title": "ei or ie?",
          "body": "Two letter pairs that trip up English speakers:\n\n- **ei** sounds like English *eye*: *mein, drei, nein*\n- **ie** sounds like English *ee*: *vier, Liebe, sie*\n\nTrick: say the English name of the **second** letter. In *ei* that's *i* (eye); in *ie* it's *e* (ee).",
          "mouthShapes": [
            { "shape": "OPEN", "label": "ei", "caption": "Start open, then slide to a smile: eye" },
            { "shape": "SPREAD", "label": "ie", "caption": "Lips wide and still: see" }
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "text": "drei", "translation": "three", "hint": "ei = eye" },
            { "text": "vier", "translation": "four", "hint": "ie = ee" },
            { "text": "nein", "translation": "no" },
            { "text": "Liebe", "translation": "love" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e2",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Wein", "Wien"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e3",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Reise", "Riese"],
          "answerIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e4",
          "type": "LISTEN_CHOOSE",
          "options": ["Leid", "Lied"],
          "answerIndex": 1
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e5",
          "type": "TEACH",
          "title": "eu, äu and au",
          "body": "- **eu** and **äu** both sound like the *oy* in *boy*: *neu, Deutsch, Häuser*\n- **au** sounds like the *ow* in *how*: *Haus, Frau*",
          "mouthShapes": [
            { "shape": "OPEN", "label": "au", "caption": "Start open, then round: how" },
            { "shape": "ROUNDED", "label": "eu / äu", "caption": "Start round, then spread: boy" }
          ],
          "examples": [
            { "text": "neu", "translation": "new" },
            { "text": "Deutsch", "translation": "German" },
            { "text": "Haus", "translation": "house" },
            { "text": "Häuser", "translation": "houses" }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e6",
          "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
          "instruction": "Which English sound matches the eu?",
          "prompt": "Deutsch",
          "promptLanguage": "TARGET",
          "options": ["oy as in boy", "you as in youth", "ow as in how"],
          "optionsLanguage": "BASE",
          "answerIndex": 0
        },
        {
          "id": "s2-e7",
          "type": "MULTIPLE_CHOICE",
